<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jelgava</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Mitau</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jelgava, known in German as Mitau, is a city in central Latvia on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lielupe">Lielupe</a> river. It grew around a castle built by the Livonian Order in 1265 and was the capital of the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/courland">Courland</a> and Semigallia from 1578 to 1795, then of the Russian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/governorate">Governorate</a> of Courland. Its baroque palace was built for Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ernst-johann-von-biron">Ernst Johann von Biron</a> by Francesco Bartolomeo Rastrelli.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jelgava.jpg" alt="Jelgava" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ivarsveilins · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jelgava_winter.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
